#### Article 3: In this law, the following terms mean

- abortion: the act of using means or substances intended to cause the premature expulsion of the fetus or, more generally, the artificial termination of pregnancy in a woman;
- harassment: the act of giving orders, using words, gestures, writings, messages, and this repeatedly, uttering threats, imposing constraints, exerting pressure or using any other means for the purpose of obtaining from a person in a situation of vulnerability or subordination favors of any nature whatsoever including sexual favors for one's own benefit or for the benefit of a third party against the will of the harassed woman;
- incest: sexual intercourse perpetrated on a person with whom one has kinship ties up to and including the third degree;
- incitement of minors to debauchery: the act of acting upon minors with a view to satisfying the passions of others or in any case as an intermediary agent of corruption and debauchery;
- forced marriage: any marriage or cohabitation contracted or decided without the free and informed consent of both parties concerned;
- female genital mutilation: all interventions including the partial or total removal of the external genital organs of a woman or injury to female genital organs performed for cultural or religious reasons or for any other non-therapeutic reason;
- pedophilia: erotic attraction of an adult towards children. More specifically, an adult who engages in touching or maintains sexual relations with a minor;

- harmful traditional practices against women: acts derived from customs and traditions that harm women. These include in particular:
	• dietary prohibitions in case of pregnancy or childbirth,
	• force-feeding which consists of excessively feeding minor girls with a view to making them physically fit for marriage,
	• degrading widowhood rites,
	• infringements on women's freedom of movement,
	• pressure on women through children.
- forced prostitution: the act of causing one or more persons to perform one or more acts of a sexual nature, by force, by threat of force or coercion or by taking advantage of the inability of said persons to give their consent freely with a view to obtaining a pecuniary or other advantage;
- procuring: the activity of one who facilitates the debauchery of others by serving as an intermediary, thereby deriving benefit from the proceeds of this activity;
- forced sterilization: the act of committing or causing to be committed on a woman without her free consent or without a justified medical decision, an act likely to deprive her of her biological and organic capacity for reproduction;
- rape: any act of vaginal, anal or oral penetration by another person's sex organ or vaginal or anal penetration by any object without the intelligent and voluntary consent of the person penetrated. However, consent is not valid for minor women under sixteen (16) years of age.
The person penetrated is not obliged to fight against their aggressor.
Being married to the person penetrated is not an excuse for the crime of rape;
- violence in the family environment: abuse of power or intentional neglect with a view to dominating, subjugating, controlling or assaulting women physically, verbally, psychologically, patrimonially,

economically and sexually, inside as well as outside the family home, whether the aggressor is linked or has been linked to his victim by kinship relations, by blood or by marriage, by marriage relations, cohabitation or whether he maintains or has maintained a de facto relationship with her;
- violence in the professional environment: refusal, in violation of the law, to hire the victim by contract, to maintain their employment or to respect general working conditions; depreciation of work performed, threats, intimidation, humiliation, exploitation and any type of distraction based on sex;
- domestic violence: any physical or sexual violence committed by a person against the person of another when the two parties are married, cohabiting, or consanguineous, or when they live in the same house, or when the two parties have had an intimate relationship in the past, but are no longer together;
- economic violence: the act of using one's means to slow down or prevent the economic or financial development of any person, or the act of preventing any person from enjoying their socio-economic rights;
- patrimonial violence: any act or neglect affecting the survival of the victim and consisting of transforming, removing, destroying, retaining or misappropriating objects, personal documents, goods and valuables, patrimonial rights or economic resources intended to cover their needs and which may extend to damage caused to common or personal property of the victim;
- physical violence: any type of act resulting in non-accidental harm, through the use of physical force or any type of weapon or object that may or may not cause internal, external or both types of injuries;
- psychological or moral violence: the act of subjecting any person to repeated actions or words having as their object or effect a degradation of living conditions likely to infringe upon their rights and dignity, to impair their physical or mental health or to compromise their plans or future.
It is also an act or neglect causing prejudice to psychological stability, abandonment, repeated inattention, excessive jealousy, insults and humiliation, devaluation,

marginalization, lack of affection, indifference, infidelity, destructive comparisons, rejection, restriction of self-determination and threats; all situations that may lead the victim to sink into depression, to isolate themselves, to lose self-esteem, or even to commit suicide;
- sexual violence: any act having the effect of degrading or causing harm to the body and/or sexuality of the victim and which, consequently, infringes upon their freedom, dignity and physical integrity.
It is the expression of an abuse of power dictated by the supremacy of man over woman who is thus denigrated and treated as an object; all other similar forms that harm or infringe upon the dignity, integrity or freedom of women;
- zoophilia: the act for any person by cunning, violence, threats or by any form of coercion or artifice, of forcing a person to have sexual relations with an animal.

## TITLE II

AWARENESS-RAISING, PREVENTION AND DETECTION MEASURES

### CHAPTER I

THE EDUCATIONAL DOMAIN

#### Article 4

Schooling is compulsory for all children without distinction of sex, race or religion up to the age of sixteen (16) years.
